From: moWerk <mo@mowerk.net>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_vhci: do not disconnect links on system suspend

hci_register_dev() registers hci_suspend_notifier() for every controller
that lacks HCI_QUIRK_NO_SUSPEND_NOTIFIER. On PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE it runs
BT_SUSPEND_DISCONNECT and drops every connection.

On a watch whose controller is driven by the vendor HAL through
bluebinder and /dev/vhci, that drops the paired phone at every suspend
attempt. The phone reconnects on the next wake, the reconnect wakes the
display, and the watch never stays asleep with a phone connected.
Measured on sol: 50 "terminated by local host for suspend" disconnects
in 25 minutes.

Set the quirk for vhci devices, as hci_h5 does for its controllers that
must not be touched at suspend.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [device-specific workaround; reported to AsteroidOS]

--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c	2026-11-09 03:04:00.587241915 +0100
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_vhci.c	2026-11-09 03:04:00.599277534 +0100
@@ -126,6 +126,14 @@
 
 	set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_VALID_LE_STATES, &hdev->quirks);
 
+	/* The controller behind this device is run by a userspace HAL
+	 * (bluebinder), which keeps connections alive across system suspend
+	 * itself. Without this quirk the suspend notifier disconnects every
+	 * link on each PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE, so a paired phone is dropped at
+	 * every suspend attempt and reconnects on the next wake.
+	 */
+	set_bit(HCI_QUIRK_NO_SUSPEND_NOTIFIER, &hdev->quirks);
+
 	if (hci_register_dev(hdev) < 0) {
 		BT_ERR("Can't register HCI device");
 		hci_free_dev(hdev);
